diagnosis_text:
Router4 is up, routing, and forwarding normally. From a PC at Location C (pc11), the default gateway 192.168.200.1 responds to ICMP with 0% loss, and ARP resolution is present. Router4’s LAN interface (eth0) is up with 192.168.200.1/24. IP forwarding is enabled (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1). Routing is active via RIP: router4 is exchanging routes on 192.168.15.0/24 and 172.25.1.0/24, and its routing table contains multiple remote subnets learned from neighbors. Router4 can ping both adjacent routers (192.168.15.1 and 172.25.1.2). Critically, all PCs at Location C (pc11–pc14) can reach an external site host (172.16.10.4) with 0% loss, proving egress works. The reported symptom (“gateway dead” and “no reachability out”) does not match current conditions.

Affected component: none; the L3 gateway and upstream routing are functioning. Mechanism: successful ICMP/ARP on the LAN and valid RIP-learned routes explain observed connectivity. Localization: router4 eth0 (192.168.200.1), eth4 (192.168.15.2), and eth5 (172.25.1.1) all operational; RIP on router4/router5 is healthy. Root cause: no network fault found; likely a transient endpoint issue or misreport. Corrective fix: no reboot required. Advise users to verify their host configs (IP/mask/gateway 192.168.200.1), clear stale ARP/DNS caches, and re-test. If an issue persists to specific remote subnets, provide exact targets so we can trace routes further, but the core gateway and intersite routing are currently healthy. 
